  <!-- SECTION 1 -->
  <section id="overview-uk-time-zones" class="content-section">
    <h2>1. Overview of United Kingdom Time Zones</h2>
    <p>
      The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland operates under a streamlined dual-time system across its primary national territory. Located on the western edge of Europe and straddling the Prime Meridian (0° Longitude), the UK serves as the historic anchor for global timekeeping.
    </p>
    <p>
      During the colder half of the year, the UK observes <a href="/blog/what-is-gmt/">Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</a>, which has an offset of UTC+0. During the warmer half of the year, the country shifts to <a href="/bst-to-est/">British Summer Time (BST)</a>, advancing clocks by one hour to UTC+1. This seasonal adjustment provides longer daylight hours during summer evenings, supporting recreation, commerce, and outdoor activities.
    </p>
    <p>
      For those coordinating international communication or comparing time zones worldwide, understanding the UK's relationship to standard references such as <a href="/gmt-vs-utc/">GMT vs UTC</a> and <a href="/gmt-to-est/">GMT to EST</a> is fundamental.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 2 -->
  <section id="history-greenwich-mean-time" class="content-section">
    <h2>2. History of Greenwich Mean Time &amp; The Royal Observatory</h2>
    <p>
      The story of Greenwich Mean Time is intrinsically tied to the history of navigation, railway expansion, and scientific astronomy. In 1675, King Charles II founded the <a href="/london-time/">Royal Observatory at Greenwich</a> to resolve the problem of finding longitude at sea.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before the mid-19th century, almost every town in Great Britain maintained its own local solar time based on the sun passing the local meridian. For example, local solar time in Bristol was approximately 10 minutes behind London, while Exeter was 14 minutes behind. With the rapid expansion of the British railway network in the 1840s, mismatched local times created severe scheduling confusion and collision risks.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 1847, the Railway Clearing House declared Greenwich Mean Time as the standard time for all railway stations across Great Britain. By 1880, GMT was officially codified into civil law via the Definition of Time Act. Four years later, at the International Meridian Conference in 1884, delegates from 25 nations voted to establish Greenwich as the Prime Meridian of the world.
    </p>

    <div class="callout" style="background: var(--panel); border-left: 4px solid #2563eb; padding: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
      <h4>Historical Benchmark: The Summer Time Act of 1916</h4>
      <p>
        During World War I, Parliament enacted the Summer Time Act 1916 following Germany's decision to adopt daylight saving time to conserve coal reserves. William Willett, a builder from Kent, had campaigned tirelessly for "Daylight Saving" starting in 1907. Although Willett passed away in 1915 before seeing his vision adopted, the UK instituted BST in May 1916.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3 -->
  <section id="daylight-saving-time-bst" class="content-section">
    <h2>3. British Summer Time (BST) &amp; DST Rules</h2>
    <p>
      Daylight Saving Time in the UK is governed by European standard harmonized rules and local statutory instruments. The exact schedule for changing clocks in the UK follows a fixed pattern:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Spring Shift (BST Begins):</strong> On the <em>last Sunday of March</em> at 01:00 UTC, clocks advance by 1 hour to 02:00 BST.</li>
      <li><strong>Autumn Shift (BST Ends):</strong> On the <em>last Sunday of October</em> at 02:00 BST, clocks fall back by 1 hour to 01:00 GMT.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Upcoming DST Transition Schedule for the UK</h3>
    <table class="data-table" style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="background: var(--panel); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">
          <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left;">Year</th>
          <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left;">Spring Transition (Clocks Forward +1h)</th>
          <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left;">Autumn Transition (Clocks Back -1h)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
