          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">No DST</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Isle of Man, Jersey, Guernsey</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">GMT (UTC+0)</td>
          <td style="padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">Yes (BST, UTC+1)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <section id="regional-variations-uk" class="content-section">
    <h2>5. Regional Solar Variations Across Great Britain &amp; NI</h2>
    <p>
      Even though the civil time is uniform across the United Kingdom, natural solar time varies significantly due to geographic spread:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Easternmost Point (Ness Point, Lowestoft, England):</strong> Located at 1°45' E longitude, local solar time is approximately 7 minutes ahead of Greenwich Mean Time.</li>
      <li><strong>Westernmost Point (Belleek, Fermanagh, Northern Ireland):</strong> Located at 8°10' W longitude, solar time is about 32 minutes behind GMT.</li>
      <li><strong>Northern Latitudes (Shetland &amp; Highlands):</strong> In Lerwick, Shetland (60°N latitude), summer day length exceeds 18 hours, giving rise to the famous "Simmer Dim" twilight, while mid-winter daylight lasts under 6 hours.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <section id="uk-vs-world-comparison" class="content-section">
    <h2>6. UK vs World Time Zone Comparison Table</h2>
    <p>
      To assist with international travel and cross-border scheduling, the table below highlights time differences between the UK (London) and key economic centers around the world during both winter and summer periods:
    </p>
